<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!--
        The one module that inherits airness-parent rather than the aggregator. Every other module parents
        to the aggregator because a plugin cannot be used in the reactor that builds it. This one is not a
        module of the harness in that sense: it is a second consumer-facing parent, and inheriting the
        first is the whole of what it does. The relativePath is written out because the default would
        resolve to the aggregator in the directory above.
    -->
    <parent>
        <groupId>eu.ciechanowiec</groupId>
        <artifactId>airness-parent</artifactId>
        <version>1.0.7-SNAPSHOT</version>
        <relativePath>../airness-parent/pom.xml</relativePath>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>airness-parent-spring-boot</artifactId>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>

    <name>airness-parent-spring-boot</name>
    <description>
        The parent a consuming Spring Boot project inherits. It carries everything airness-parent carries,
        and adds the Spring version set and the rules that only a Spring Boot project can break.

        A project states which kind it is by which parent it names, rather than by a switch. That is what
        keeps the Spring relaxations off every project that is not a Spring Boot one, and what keeps them
        reviewable: they are a diff against the parent above rather than a second copy of it.

        Two rules are relaxed and five are added, so a project inheriting this answers to more than one
        inheriting airness-parent, never to less.
    </description>

    <properties>
        <!--
            The inversion of what airness-parent declares. Relaxed reaches every file, so the handful of
            filters keyed to a Spring annotation now fire, and suppressed reaches none, so the Spring
            rules stop being suppressed and apply.

            Both are read by the Checkstyle configuration airness-parent already points at. This is the
            whole of how a Spring Boot project is judged differently, and it is two values rather than a
            second rule set, so the difference can be read in one place. Neither is referenced in this
            file, which is why the property order puts them ahead of the version below.
        -->
        <airness.checkstyle.spring.relaxed>.*</airness.checkstyle.spring.relaxed>
        <airness.checkstyle.spring.suppressed>^$</airness.checkstyle.spring.suppressed>
        <!--
            The Spring Boot release the harness pins. A consumer names no Spring version at all: the bill
            of materials below manages every coordinate the platform ships, and check-parameters refuses a
            project that declares this property for itself.

            Pinned here rather than in the root pom because no other module resolves a Spring coordinate,
            and a bill of materials imported at the root would be inherited by airness-parent as well,
            which is how a plain Java project would start silently resolving a dependency it declared no
            version for.
        -->
        <spring-boot.version>4.1.1</spring-boot.version>
    </properties>

    <!--
        Spring Framework reads JSpecify annotations reflectively while it builds beans, so the classes
        have to be in the archive that ships. airness-parent declares them provided, which is right for a
        library whose annotations are read by a compiler and by nothing afterwards, and wrong here: the
        repackaged application starts, gets as far as the first handler mapping, and dies on a
        NoClassDefFoundError naming an annotation nobody wrote.

        Redeclared rather than changed above, because a plain Java project has no such runtime and would
        be shipping an annotation jar it never loads.
    -->
    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.jspecify</groupId>
            <artifactId>jspecify</artifactId>
            <scope>compile</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <dependencyManagement>
        <dependencies>
            <!--
                Ahead of the platform below, because the first match in a dependencyManagement block wins
                and these two both manage JUnit. Without this the harness's own pin would reach the
                Jupiter artifacts while the platform artifacts underneath them came from Spring, and the
                two halves of one test framework would be at different versions. That fails at run time,
                inside the reflection Jupiter does across the seam, with a message about neither pin.

                Airness owns the test framework rather than deferring to the platform here, because the
                timeout, the ordering seed, and the assertion rules are all written against the version
                it pins. The version property is the one the root pom already declares.
            -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>org.junit</groupId>
                <artifactId>junit-bom</artifactId>
                <version>${junit.version}</version>
                <type>pom</type>
                <scope>import</scope>
            </dependency>
            <!--
                The platform bill of materials. Imported rather than depended on, so a consumer declares a
                starter with a scope and no version, exactly as it already declares JUnit.

                One versioned declaration standing for the whole platform is also what keeps the freshness
                bound meaningful here. That check reads declarations that carry a version, and a consumer
                whose starters take their versions from a bill of materials declares none, so without this
                coordinate the whole Spring graph would go unread rather than be reported as current.
            -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
                <artifactId>spring-boot-dependencies</artifactId>
                <version>${spring-boot.version}</version>
                <type>pom</type>
                <scope>import</scope>
            </dependency>
        </dependencies>
    </dependencyManagement>

    <build>
        <pluginManagement>
            <plugins>
                <!--
                    Version only, and no execution. Repackaging is what turns a module into an executable
                    archive, and which module that is belongs to the project: a reactor holding one
                    application and three libraries wants it on one of the four. So the harness pins the
                    version and the project binds the goal, which is the same division ManagedVersions
                    already draws for every plugin it allows rather than supplies.

                    The archive a repackaging project ships is still read. artifact-content runs in verify
                    rather than package precisely so that it opens the archive this plugin rewrote.
                -->
                <plugin>
                    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
                    <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                    <version>${spring-boot.version}</version>
                </plugin>
            </plugins>
        </pluginManagement>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <!--
                    A starter declares no type of its own, so every one of them reads as a dependency
                    nothing uses, and every Spring type a project does use arrives through one and reads
                    as undeclared. Both halves of that are what a starter is for, so both are named here
                    rather than left to be worked around per project.

                    The children are appended rather than written over the inherited list, which is what
                    keeps the airness-annotations exemption airness-parent declares.
                -->
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-dependency-plugin</artifactId>
                <configuration>
                    <ignoredUnusedDeclaredDependencies combine.children="append">
                        <ignoredUnusedDeclaredDependency>
                            org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter*
                        </ignoredUnusedDeclaredDependency>
                        <!-- Supplied above for the container to read, rather than for this project's code. -->
                        <ignoredUnusedDeclaredDependency>org.jspecify:jspecify</ignoredUnusedDeclaredDependency>
                    </ignoredUnusedDeclaredDependencies>
                    <ignoredUsedUndeclaredDependencies combine.children="append">
                        <ignoredUsedUndeclaredDependency>org.springframework:*</ignoredUsedUndeclaredDependency>
                        <ignoredUsedUndeclaredDependency>org.springframework.boot:*</ignoredUsedUndeclaredDependency>
                    </ignoredUsedUndeclaredDependencies>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <!--
                    The questions that only a Spring Boot project has to answer, and that no analyzer
                    configuration can ask. Bound here rather than in airness-parent, so a project that is
                    not a Spring Boot one never runs the goal at all.

                    The four goals differ in what each has to read before it can answer. Two read one file
                    at a time, one reads every source of the module because the halves of the defect it
                    looks for are written in different files, and the last reads every source of the build
                    because a second application class is only a defect relative to the first.
                -->
                <groupId>eu.ciechanowiec</groupId>
                <artifactId>airness-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>airness-spring-governance</id>
                        <phase>package</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>spring-source</goal>
                            <goal>spring-configuration</goal>
                            <goal>spring-module</goal>
                            <goal>spring-reactor</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
